text:
{
  "decision": "revise",
  "headline": "Custom-order check trims NVIDIA bull to $391.79bn; base $374.62bn and Q3 $103bn held",
  "note": "The October 1–8 synthetic check reports larger 2027 orders from three ODM/OEM respondents, rising custom-accelerator order share at two unnamed clouds, and one planner whose deployment pace depends on power [channel-oct2026]. I compared it with AWS’s February production disclosures and April/July Trainium commitments. Those primary sources independently support custom adoption; they do not verify the unnamed clouds or incremental dollar shares [amzn-2026-02-05-release] [amzn-2026-04-29-release] [amzn-2026-07-30-release].\n\nThis is a distinct document/interview channel, but respondent independence and order overlap remain unverified. I count one check, not eight corroborations. I halve NVIDIA’s optional bull capture premium over the 65% base: 66% becomes 65.5%, a judgment haircut that lowers calendar 2026 bull revenue $2.99bn to $391.79bn. The check mainly concerns 2027; it changes my confidence in favorable share persistence, not measured 2026 share.\n\nPower readiness could stage deployment despite larger orders, but no dated slip is established. Stable neocloud lead times are not evidence of deterioration. I therefore retain $682.5bn capex, $103bn Q3 Data Center, own Q4 $128bn and AMD base $14.00bn. I partly disagree with the vendor’s “share, not volume” conclusion: energized volume also depends on power.\n\nNVIDIA’s long capture thesis weakens slightly in the upside; its base street gap stays +$2.62bn (+0.70%), while the bull gap narrows from +$22.78bn to +$19.79bn.",
  "stance": "Capex remains modestly above street; custom silicon limits NVIDIA upside, while power readiness makes 2027 orders a timing question.",
  "rationale": "New interviews add directional order-mix evidence beyond prior corporate Trainium disclosures, with independence unverified. The unquantified, largely 2027 evidence warrants removing half of the one-percentage-point NVIDIA bull share premium, rather than assuming a measured 2026 share loss. This changes only bull nvda_share from 0.66 to 0.655. No evidence establishes a 2026 guide change, dated shipment slip, AMD GPU revenue or customer concentration change.",
  "thesis": "Calendar 2026 capex remains $682.5bn versus $667.5bn synthetic street; NVIDIA base remains $374.62bn versus $372bn and AMD GPU base $14.00bn versus $14bn. NVIDIA bear/base/bull spend shares are now 64%/65%/65.5%, yielding Data Center revenue $351.34bn/$374.62bn/$391.79bn. All other inputs are held. Larger 2027 orders support demand persistence but do not determine realized capex, supplier dollars or energized capacity. The anonymous custom-order signal reduces upside capture confidence; power-related deployment slippage remains unresolved. Unreported NVIDIA Q3 FY27 stays $103bn, expected November 18 unconfirmed; own Q4 stays $128bn versus $129.86bn required.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "statement": "The synthetic vendor reports interviews with eight respondents across supply, power/cooling, neocloud procurement and one hyperscaler planner; it does not identify them.",
